# Line endings are LF. Not "should be" -- are.
#
# .editorconfig asks editors nicely; this file makes git enforce it. That is the
# difference between a convention and a guarantee: `text=auto eol=lf` normalises
# to LF in the index *and* checks out LF on Windows, so a contributor with
# core.autocrlf=true cannot commit CRLF by accident.
#
# Why this matters in a Rust repository specifically: rustfmt does not normalise
# line endings. A CRLF file passes `cargo fmt --check` on the machine that wrote
# it and then shows up as a whole-file diff for the next person. Three files
# (.editorconfig, .gitattributes, .prettierrc) agree on LF so that never happens.

* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Diff drivers ------------------------------------------------------------
# `diff=rust` is a git built-in: hunk headers show the enclosing `fn` or `impl`
# instead of a bare line number, which makes every `git diff` and every review
# slightly easier to read. Free, and almost nobody turns it on.
#
# There is no built-in driver for TOML, so Cargo.toml diffs stay generic.
*.rs   diff=rust
*.md   diff=markdown

# --- The exceptions to LF ----------------------------------------------------
# Batch and PowerShell interpreters can genuinely misbehave on LF-only files.
# These are the only files in a repository that should ever contain CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# --- Generated, but committed ------------------------------------------------
# Cargo.lock is committed on purpose (see the README) and reviewed by nobody.
# `linguist-generated` collapses it by default in GitHub pull request diffs and
# keeps it out of the repository's language statistics. It does not hide the
# file from `git diff`, and it does not stop you reviewing it when you want to.
Cargo.lock text eol=lf linguist-generated=true

# --- Binary: never normalise, never diff -------------------------------------
# Without these, `text=auto` guesses. It guesses well, but a false positive
# corrupts the file, and the corruption is committed before anyone notices.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.wasm binary

# --- Note on `export-ignore` -------------------------------------------------
# `path/ export-ignore` omits a path from `git archive` tarballs. Deliberately
# not used here: it affects only `git archive`, NOT `cargo package`, which uses
# the `include`/`exclude` keys in Cargo.toml. Setting it and assuming crates.io
# honours it is a common and quiet mistake.
